The documented surface of `hog config` (HLD §6). A verb that is renamed, added
or dropped changes this file, and that is the point: the five verbs below are
the whole of it, and each one has to describe itself in one line.

The surface is subcommands, not flags (HLD §11.8). The v0.2 flags `-e`, `-d`,
`--path` and `--init` are gone rather than deprecated, and `tests/cli_surface.rs`
pins that each of them is now a usage error.

The runner pins `COLUMNS`, `HOG_CONFIG` and `NO_COLOR` (see the test that
loads this file): clap wraps to the terminal width and echoes `$HOG_CONFIG`
into the `--config` row, so without them this file would record whichever
machine last ran it.

```console
$ hog config --help
Inspect and edit the config file.

With no verb it prints the resolved configuration and the path it came from. Every verb below names
one thing to look at or change.

Usage: hog config [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  path     Print the path of the config file that is actually read
  init     Write a commented starter config if none exists
  edit     Open the config file in $VISUAL or $EDITOR
  exclude  Show the persistent exclude list, or change it
  command  Show the command template, or change it
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          Path to the config file (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml)
          
          [env: HOG_CONFIG=]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```

`exclude` and `command` are the two verbs that take an operand, and a bare verb
is the read-only form of each.

```console
$ hog config exclude --help
Show the persistent exclude list, or change it

Usage: hog config exclude [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  add   Append FIELDs to the exclude list, keeping every comment in the file
  rm    Remove FIELDs from the exclude list
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --config <PATH>  Path to the config file (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml) [env:
                       HOG_CONFIG=]
  -h, --help           Print help

```

```console
$ hog config command set --help
Write a new command template, after checking that it works.

The template is split by shell rules and its placeholders are checked before anything is written, so
a template that could only fail at the next run never reaches the file (HLD §3).

Usage: hog config command set [OPTIONS] <TEMPLATE>

Arguments:
  <TEMPLATE>
          The template, e.g. `ssh {0} 'docker logs -f {@}'`.
          
          `allow_hyphen_values` is on: a template is a command line, and one starting with a flag
          (`--follow …`) is not a hog flag.

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          Path to the config file (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml)
          
          [env: HOG_CONFIG=]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
